Malcolm Ryan wrote:
> As the next step in my ongoing project to add an Alchemist class to the
> game, I am creating the Philosopher's Stone. My aim is to produce an
> artifact which promotes the use of alchemy without replacing it. I'm
> currently considering:
> 
> * It gives hungerless regen when carried (giving "eternal youth")
> * It creates potions of acid when invoked (promoting gem-alchemy and
> providing ammo)
> * It operates as a unihorn when applied/dipped.
> 
> What do you think? Is this too weak? Too strong?
> I figure the unihorn bit is no big deal. Any player worth her salt
> should already have a unihorn before the quest anyway. But it is
> fitting that the stone should have these kinds of powers.
> 
> It has also been suggested that it could be used to turn rocks (or
> maybe metal objects) into gold pieces. It fits the mythos, but I'm not
> sure it's a very interesting power.
> 
> Malcolm
> 
One of the prime functions of the Philosopher's stone is transmutation 
(of lead) into gold. Applying the stone to a metal item should turn the 
metal into gold pieces. This would be useful when Fort Ludios is not 
generated; and you lost your protection (thru Yet Another Stupid Move).

The unihorn ability is more of a healer function than an Alchemy function.

Dipping the stone into any potion except acid should convert the potion 
to acid.

UNLESS, a different mythos is implemented. From "FullMetal Alchemist", 
"The obtained Stone is not bound to a fixed form". In Nethack, 
implementation would mean the Stone randomly changes gem types. This 
would allow for a greater implementation of the gem alchemy you 
mentioned. The Alchemist would have to keep looking at his stone to see 
if the Stone had transmuted itself into a form which was useful.

The following quote I think is quite the flavor of Nethack:
Hence the saying "those who seek the Philosopher's Stone are destined to 
die".

FullMetal Alchemist has a few other fun twists:
1)Excessive use of the Stone turns you into a Zombie. DYWYPI?
2)Even a true Philosopher's Stone is not immune to Equivalent Exchange: 
every time it is used, a bit of it vanishes, and its power is likewise 
reduced.


